WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.639
I’ve always hated shopping for furniture.

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.639
Grocery shopping?

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:05.609
They’ve got cake, cheese, and beer.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:06.640
Sounds perfect to me.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.640
Clothes shopping?

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.860
Sure, I’ll pick up some new jeans, that’s
a fine Saturday afternoon.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.750
But shopping for household items, pushing
through the crowds of broke college students

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:19.980
and bickering couples on a hunt for coffee
tables and tv stands, all 20-page instruction

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.369
books, a dozen wooden puzzle pieces, and tiny
little screws that disappear into your carpet

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:28.660
the second you take them out of the package…that’s
my own personal hell.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.611
So, when my wife Brenda, the love of my life,
my light in the darkest of times, asked me

00:00:33.611 --> 00:00:39.340
to go to Ikea with her to pick up the GLOSTAD
sofa she found online, plus a few other essentials

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.050
for the new house, I was reluctant, to say
the least.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.440
But love won out, and I just couldn’t say
no to those eyes - or the promise that we’d

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.620
pick up my favorite takeout on the way home.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.230
It would be a quick trip.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.170
We run in, we grab the sofa and maybe a HYLLIS
shelving unit for her office, and we head

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:57.260
back out.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.730
One hour total, tops.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.230
Well, some mad god of chaos and cheap furniture
must have turned his hateful eye on me, because

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.230
it’s been over a day and I’m still here.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.180
I’ve been walking for hours, and nothing
has changed.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.049
I knew Ikea was big, but this seems impossible.

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:11.550
It’s like the store never ends.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:16.380
I keep calling out her name, “Brenda!”
but there’s no response.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.929
Just my own voice bouncing off the flat white
surface of the rows of MUSKEN wardrobes.

00:01:20.929 --> 00:01:21.979
I was such a jerk.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:25.560
I always do that, get swept up in the stress
when we go furniture shopping.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.149
I told myself I wouldn’t this time, but
like clockwork, I got overwhelmed by the vast

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:34.520
aisles, the myriad of options, the hard-to-pronounce
names.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.520
I was impatient.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.280
I told her to go look at throw pillows on
her own, because I was sick of getting sidetracked.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.280
She loved throw pillows.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.280
No, no.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.770
I can’t let myself do that, it hasn’t
been that long.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.060
She’s got to be here somewhere.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.799
I can’t let the last time we spoke be a
fight about living room décor.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.270
Day Two
So, it’s my second day lost in Ikea.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.209
Can’t believe I just wrote that.

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:00.450
Also, I’m not entirely sure how many days
it’s been.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.009
There are no windows here, I can’t see when
the sun’s up and when it’s down.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:09.349
The lights turn off sometimes, and I think
that’s meant to indicate night?

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.260
But I really can’t be sure.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.900
The clock on my phone is stuck at 3:22 PM,
the exact time we entered the store.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.400
I guess I should say, I’ve slept once since
being here.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.909
Managed to camp out on a JESSHEIM futon mattress.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:26.349
It wasn’t the most comfortable, but I’ve
definitely had worse.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:29.430
This place reminds me of the Island of the
Lotus Eaters in The Odyssey.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.180
I can’t tell how much time is passing.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.189
Days and nights don’t really exist here.

00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:37.819
Speaking of eating, I wish I had some food
right about now.

00:02:37.819 --> 00:02:41.290
Brenda always has granola bars in her purse,
she’s ready for anything.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.049
I’ve always admired that about her.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:47.799
I had a piece of gum in my pocket but I think
it lost its flavor ages ago.

00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:52.830
Nothing to eat, no one to talk to…I haven’t
seen an employee, another customer, not a

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.170
single living soul.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.659
I don’t have any signal, but my phone still
has battery, and my step counter app says

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:00.540
I’ve walked thirty miles.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.540
Thirty.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.540
Miles.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.459
And still not one friendly face, no exit sign…

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:09.569
I just had the most horrible thought, as I
sit here on this pile of STOENSE scribbling

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:10.569
in this notebook.

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:11.769
What if I’m dead?

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:17.049
What if I died on the store floor, keeled
over from a heart attack or a stroke or something,

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:19.140
and this is my eternal resting place?

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.910
I know I wasn’t perfect, I could have done
more charity work, volunteered at soup kitchens,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.209
and helped more old ladies cross the street,
but I didn’t think I’d wind up in Hell.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.590
The philosopher Jean-Paul Sartre once wrote,
“Hell is other people.”

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.840
But I’d like to respectfully disagree with
Monsieur Sartre.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.359
Hell is an endless, vacant Ikea.
Day Three

00:03:39.359 --> 00:03:42.800
So it turns out that I’m not alone here.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.019
Not exactly.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:47.989
I still haven’t seen another human being,
but I did see something else.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.560
While I was trying to sleep, nestled in a
makeshift bed of FJÄDRAR cushion covers

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.420
and kept awake by the gnawing growl of my
empty stomach, I heard a shuffling sound several

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.560
feet away.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.560
Footsteps.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.330
I sat up, eyes darting around for the source
of the sound.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.330
Could it be Brenda?

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.140
Or some other hapless soul sucked into this
prison with me?

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:12.090
I spotted the silhouette of a person, wearing
an Ikea employee uniform, that unmistakable

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.689
bright yellow shirt - a beacon of hope.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:20.079
Then it turned around, and I realized this
was no ordinary salesperson.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:22.759
Their face was smooth, featureless, like an
egg.

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:27.460
If an egg was the size of a human head, and
perched on top of a seven-foot frame.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.990
Once I noticed its face, or lack thereof,
I took in the rest of the creature’s nightmarish

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:32.990
form.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:38.270
Its proportions were all off; short, stubby
legs beneath a distended torso that stretched

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:42.389
into long, long arms that dragged along the
ground as it walked.

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:47.990
Its hands were massive, with tapered fingers
perfect for grabbing anyone who gets too close.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.200
Well, there was no way in hell I was falling
asleep with that thing roaming around, so

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.970
I got moving and tried to sneak my way past
it.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.430
I thought I was in the clear when suddenly…

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.470
”Excuse me, sir?”

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:04.000
A voice came from the direction of the monster,
even though it had no mouth to speak with.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.370
“The store is closed.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.490
You need to vacate the premises.”

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.830
It spoke in a pleasant tone, polite.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.910
A true “customer service” voice.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.000
But when I turned, it was ripping its way
through the maze of furniture toward me like

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.120
it wanted to tear me limb from limb.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.759
I haven’t done much running since my high
school track and field days, but I turned

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.500
and booked it as fast as I could.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.330
Didn’t matter that it was dark, didn’t
matter that I could trip at any second and

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.800
land face-first on a lamp or the sharp corner
of a table.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.160
I had to get as far away from that thing as
possible before I found out first-hand what

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.150
exactly it did to trespassers.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:47.270
But my stamina isn’t what it used to be,
and I could feel exhaustion catching up to

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:48.270
me.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.759
I could hear the thundering of the creature’s
footsteps as it gave chase, and I knew my

00:05:51.759 --> 00:05:54.360
heart couldn’t keep this up too much longer.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.800
So I did what I used to do when I was a kid
and the big neighbor boys tried to beat the

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.669
crap out of me on my walk home: I hid.

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:05.349
I yanked open the doors to a wardrobe and
I stuffed myself in, holding the doors closed

00:06:05.349 --> 00:06:06.419
from the inside.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:11.169
I could hear a scrabbling sound against the
door, the scraping and scratching of the staff

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:13.280
monster trying to get at me through the wood.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.770
But thank the Swedes for their fine engineering,
because that wood just would not give.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.320
After what felt like an eternity, the assault
on the door stopped, and I could hear the

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.580
creature shuffling away, off to another section
of the massive store.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.940
I stayed there in the pitch dark for a long
time, slowing my breathing down and trying

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.639
to get my heart to climb back down from my
throat.

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:37.099
What was that thing?

00:06:37.099 --> 00:06:38.099
Did it live here?

00:06:38.099 --> 00:06:39.620
Where did its voice come from?

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.580
How did it know I was here?

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.780
The questions bounced around my mind, and
I knew I might never find the answers.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.379
I had guessed before that this was no ordinary
Ikea, the endless rows of aisles and total

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:54.979
lack of any human beings had been a pretty
good clue.

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:58.860
But now I knew for sure, I had left my own
world behind.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.400
As I drifted off to sleep, the wardrobe closing
around me like a coffin, my drifting conscious

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.669
thoughts were of Brenda; whether she was trapped
in here too, and, on the off chance she was,

00:07:09.669 --> 00:07:12.860
if those creatures would get to her before
I could.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.940
After the lights turned back on, and a new
“day” began

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:19.340
I started my walk in search of one thing only:
food.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:24.389
I had felt the hunger getting to me before,
but after my sprint away from the faceless

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:27.680
monster, my body was nearly limp with exhaustion.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.599
I had to get some calories in me as soon as
possible, or I might not have the strength

00:07:31.599 --> 00:07:33.300
to run for my life next time.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:37.570
It took a few hours of walking, but I eventually
spotted what I was looking for.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:42.320
There’s only one thing I’ve ever enjoyed
about Ikea, and it’s those beautiful little

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.409
meatballs.

00:07:43.409 --> 00:07:49.190
As I rounded a corner past a stack of VANILJSTÅNG
plant stands, the heavenly aroma hit me like

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.599
the most welcome slap to the face in the world.

00:07:51.599 --> 00:07:57.030
There, in front of me, bathed in the warm
glow of the fluorescent lights, was the store

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.759
restaurant, and in the center, a trough of
meatballs.

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:03.830
I could see the steam rising up from the little
jewels of meat.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:07.610
I didn’t care that there was no chef present,
or anyone who could have made them.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.669
I ran to the food and I stuffed my face.

00:08:10.669 --> 00:08:15.530
Once my stomach was full of meatballs and
my heart full of new hope, I examined my surroundings.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:17.099
There were snacks!

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:18.780
Swedish snacks I could bring with me.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.949
I grabbed a KLAMBY canvas bag and began to
fill it with whatever I could grab: cans of

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:28.639
cider, jars of lingonberry jam and mustard,
boxes of muesli, and bags of sour candy, hell,

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.360
I even grabbed some of the salty licorice.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.300
When food is this scarce, you don’t look
a gift horse in the mouth, even when its breath

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.770
absolutely reeks.

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.871
I’m not too sure where I’ll sleep tonight.

00:08:38.871 --> 00:08:42.289
Don’t want to be out in the open, in case
more of the staff find me.

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:46.890
I try not to think too long about what might
happen if they catch me next time.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.750
Maybe this is crazy, but I think I’ll build
a fort.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:53.620
I’ve got plenty of space for it, and plenty
of materials.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.720
I’ll climb up to the top of a tall shelf,
and build a fort up there.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.740
Just have to hope I don’t toss and turn
too much in my sleep, or I might impale myself

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.600
on a TÅGARP floor lamp.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.440
Day Four
Something incredible happened.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.450
I built my fort pretty close to the food area
so I’d be able to access it again when I

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.450
woke up.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:15.240
When the lights came on this morning, I saw
someone helping themselves to the meatballs.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.070
It couldn’t be staff, because they’ve
got nowhere to put the food even if they did

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:20.070
eat.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.940
I took a closer look, and it was a person,
an actual human person!

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:28.440
A blonde woman in a blue sweater, loading
up a bag with meatballs and other supplies

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.010
just like I had the previous day.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:31.010
“Hey!”

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.440
I called out to her, and my voice felt strange.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.310
Rusty from not talking to anyone for days,
I guess.

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.779
She spun around, brandishing a fire axe.

00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:41.810
Right, she probably thought I was a staff
member preparing to attack.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:46.080
I waved my arms to draw attention to my face,
its eyes, nose, and mouth, to make it clear

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:47.210
I wasn’t one of them.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:48.210
“Sorry!

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:49.350
Sorry, I’m human!

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:50.350
Hello!”

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.850
Her posture relaxed, and she motioned for
me to come down and speak to her.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.779
She introduced herself as Gloria, and explained
that she was part of a community of other

00:09:57.779 --> 00:10:00.589
humans who had built a town in the Checkout
department.

00:10:00.589 --> 00:10:04.850
They had set up lights, beds, and, most importantly,
a gate to keep the staff out at night.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:06.360
“How long have you been here?”

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.420
she asked me, as we made our way toward Checkouts
together.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.090
“Four days?

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:12.090
I think.”

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.480
Her eyes widened, impressed.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.950
“Most people usually don’t make it through
that many nights alone in here.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.589
The staff gets to them first.”

00:10:18.589 --> 00:10:22.210
I shuddered at her words, and my thoughts
turned once again to Brenda.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.300
I asked Gloria if she’d seen my wife at
all, if she’d made it to Checkouts before

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:27.300
me.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:28.300
She shook her head sadly.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.930
“I came in with my sister.”

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:30.930
She told me.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:31.990
“I haven’t seen her since.”

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:33.750
“And how long have you been here?”

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:34.790
I asked.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:38.709
Gloria sighed, and patted me on the back before
speaking again, “About a year.”

00:10:38.709 --> 00:10:42.910
We were silent the rest of the walk, and by
the time we reached the gates of Checkouts,

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.519
the lights were turning off for the night.

00:10:44.519 --> 00:10:46.200
“Quick, we need to get inside.”

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.180
Gloria ushered me through the gates and followed
behind.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.610
I was about to question her urgency when suddenly
two faceless horrors lurched from the shadows.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.610
These two staff members were smaller than
the one I’d seen before, maybe four feet

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:58.610
tall.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:02.070
They had the same long arms though, and they
were reaching for us as they ran at the gate

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.589
with primal speed.

00:11:03.589 --> 00:11:06.910
They threw their bodies against it, arms stretching
toward me and Gloria.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.790
I screamed, jumping back.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.470
Gloria just watched them, stone-faced, waiting
to see if this would be the night they actually

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:13.670
broke through.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:18.070
After a while, the staff gave up and stumbled
away, and I turned to take in the town of

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.070
Checkouts.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:23.709
I never thought I’d be so thrilled to see
a collection of beds, lamps, and strangers.

00:11:23.709 --> 00:11:27.720
So many people, when I had worried I’d never
see another person again!

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.930
There are around 40 people in total, though
I haven’t met everyone yet.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.680
Some folks were already sleeping when I got
there or keeping to themselves.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.800
The people I did meet nearly knocked me out
with their kindness.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.310
They fed me, gave me water, a bed to sleep
in, and a set of clean clothes one of them

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.810
had fashioned out of sheets.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.519
They’re not exactly stylish but I’d take
anything over the t-shirt and slacks I’ve

00:11:49.519 --> 00:11:51.399
been sleeping and sweating in for days.

00:11:51.399 --> 00:11:54.540
I think I could be okay here, at least for
a little while.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:57.440
At the very least, I think I’ll get a good
night’s sleep for once.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.500
Day 11
So I’ve been here at Checkouts for about

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:05.040
a week now, and as bizarre as it feels to
say this, I think I’m starting to settle

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.040
in.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.230
Being around other people keeps me sane.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:12.490
Without that endless stretch of silence, there’s
less space for my mind to wander to the worst

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:13.490
possibilities.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:17.320
John, one of the men here, had a phone charger
with him, and I was able to get mine juiced

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:18.320
up again.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:22.339
There’s still no signal, but I had a few
movies saved, and plenty of podcasts.

00:12:22.339 --> 00:12:26.580
I’ve been listening to a true crime show
with some of the other people here, distracting

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:29.889
ourselves from our own horror with the tale
of somebody else’s.

00:12:29.889 --> 00:12:34.600
We gather around, break into the rations we
gathered during the day, and even crack open

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:38.680
some of the cider, while we listen to the
silky-voiced narrator take us through the

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.000
story of bloody murder in a small town.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.010
It’s kind of like summer camp.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:47.830
You know, without the nature, or the camp
counselors, or the possibility of it ever

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:48.830
ending.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:53.350
I’ve been asking around about Brenda, describing
her, seeing if anyone’s spotted her, dead

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:54.350
or alive.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:55.570
No one has.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:59.649
Gloria said that maybe Brenda never made it
to where we are, maybe she found the exit

00:12:59.649 --> 00:13:03.500
a long time ago and she’s somewhere in the
real world waiting for me to come back.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.500
That’s what she thinks happened to her sister.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.820
It’s coming up on the one-year anniversary
of her getting trapped inside, and she told

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:10.840
me a secret.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.420
She’s planning to try and escape.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:16.060
She’s spotted exit signs before, and tracked
them across the store.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:20.890
They seem to move around, though, shifting
from one place to another when no one is watching.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:24.260
But she’s figured out a pattern, and she’s
going to put it to the test.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.860
She’s leaving next week, she wanted to tell
me.

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:29.730
She’s a nice lady, I hope she makes it.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:32.660
Day 18
I woke up to the sounds of screaming.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:37.290
I rushed to the gate to see what was going
on, and I saw John walking back from his supply

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.410
run, cradling something in his arms.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.019
I couldn’t make it out, but it was limp,
pale, and dripping red.

00:13:43.019 --> 00:13:47.680
As he got closer, I saw a head of blonde hair,
and my stomach dropped.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:48.680
Gloria.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:52.230
She had snuck out while everyone was asleep,
didn’t want to say goodbyes, and had tried

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:53.550
to find her way out.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.709
The Staff had found her first.

00:13:55.709 --> 00:14:00.089
She was dead by the time John spotted her,
lying on the ground, a crimson smear across

00:14:00.089 --> 00:14:02.910
the tile signaling where the staff had dragged
her.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:08.009
They packed her up into a box as a kind of
burial, we all said a few words, and then

00:14:08.009 --> 00:14:10.160
two of the men took the box away.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:14.399
They had to put it somewhere far away from
the town…before it started to smell.

00:14:14.399 --> 00:14:19.380
I can’t sleep, I can’t stop thinking about
Gloria and what happened to her.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:22.160
She seemed so sure…so confident.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.690
I’ve been looking through her things, what
she had left by her bed.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:29.310
There isn’t much, but there’s a notebook
like mine.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:34.029
She wrote her plan in here, everything she
noticed about the exit signs, the Staff’s

00:14:34.029 --> 00:14:38.360
behavioral patterns…I know it didn’t work
out for her, but there’s something here.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.459
No one’s spotted Brenda yet.

00:14:40.459 --> 00:14:42.510
I don’t think she’s in here with me.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:45.140
I think, somehow, she got free.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:48.949
If she’s out there waiting for me, I can’t
spend one more night here.

00:14:48.949 --> 00:14:49.949
I won’t.

00:14:49.949 --> 00:14:51.149
I’m going to go for it.

00:14:51.149 --> 00:14:53.019
I’m finding the exit tonight.

00:14:53.019 --> 00:14:56.600
Whether that’s the door out of this place
or the sweet release of death, I guess we’ll

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:57.600
just have to wait and see.

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.950
Either way, I’ll be out of this Scandinavian
modernist hellhole once and for all.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:07.630
Now go check out “SCP-3008 - Trapped in
IKEA” and “How To Actually Beat SCP-3008

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:11.600
The Infinite Ikea” for more on the deadliest
furniture store around!

